    "['set protocols ospf area [area_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco configuration for the specified Juniper OSPF commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n passive-interface loopback0\\n network 10.10.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper commands configure OSPF area 0 with three interfaces (lo0.0, ge-1/0/0.1, and ge-2/0/0.0)\\n2. In Cisco:\\n   - The loopback interface is explicitly made passive\\n   - The ge-2/0/0.0 interface's network (10.10.1.0/24) is included in OSPF area 0\\n   - The ge-1/0/0.1 interface would need its specific IP address included if it had one configured\\n   - Cisco automatically enables OSPF on interfaces that match the network statements\", 'trans_template': 'router ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco configuration for the specified Juniper OSPF commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n passive-interface loopback0\\n network 10.10.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper commands configure OSPF area 0 with three interfaces (lo0.0, ge-1/0/0.1, and ge-2/0/0.0)\\n2. In Cisco:\\n   - The loopback interface is explicitly made passive\\n   - The ge-2/0/0.0 interface's network (10.10.1.0/24) is included in OSPF area 0\\n   - The ge-1/0/0.1 interface would need its specific IP address included if it had one configured\\n   - Cisco automatically enables OSPF on interfaces that match the network statements\", 'trans_template': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'passive-interface loopback0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'passive-interface loopback0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco configuration for the specified Juniper OSPF commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n passive-interface loopback0\\n network 10.10.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper commands configure OSPF area 0 with three interfaces (lo0.0, ge-1/0/0.1, and ge-2/0/0.0)\\n2. In Cisco:\\n   - The loopback interface is explicitly made passive\\n   - The ge-2/0/0.0 interface's network (10.10.1.0/24) is included in OSPF area 0\\n   - The ge-1/0/0.1 interface would need its specific IP address included if it had one configured\\n   - Cisco automatically enables OSPF on interfaces that match the network statements\", 'trans_template': 'passive-interface loopback0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 10.10.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 10.10.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco configuration for the specified Juniper OSPF commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n passive-interface loopback0\\n network 10.10.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper commands configure OSPF area 0 with three interfaces (lo0.0, ge-1/0/0.1, and ge-2/0/0.0)\\n2. In Cisco:\\n   - The loopback interface is explicitly made passive\\n   - The ge-2/0/0.0 interface's network (10.10.1.0/24) is included in OSPF area 0\\n   - The ge-1/0/0.1 interface would need its specific IP address included if it had one configured\\n   - Cisco automatically enables OSPF on interfaces that match the network statements\", 'trans_template': 'network 10.10.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}]]": 1,
    "['interface [interface_name] passive', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper OSPF passive interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n passive-interface Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco configuration:\\n1. Starts the OSPF process (using an arbitrary process ID 1)\\n2. Marks the Loopback0 interface as passive, which prevents OSPF hello packets from being sent while still advertising the loopback's network in OSPF\\n3. This matches the Juniper behavior where lo0.0 is included in OSPF area 0 but marked as passive\", 'trans_template': 'router ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'passive-interface Loopback0', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper OSPF passive interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n passive-interface Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco configuration:\\n1. Starts the OSPF process (using an arbitrary process ID 1)\\n2. Marks the Loopback0 interface as passive, which prevents OSPF hello packets from being sent while still advertising the loopback's network in OSPF\\n3. This matches the Juniper behavior where lo0.0 is included in OSPF area 0 but marked as passive\", 'trans_template': 'passive-interface Loopback0'}]]": 1,
