    "['unit [unit_number] family [family_type] vlan members [vlan_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco IOS command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.0\\n switchport trunk allowed vlan 10\\n switchport mode trunk\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, we typically use subinterfaces with dot notation (Gi1/0/0.0)\\n2. The command enables trunking mode and allows only VLAN 10\\n3. The \"family ethernet-switching\" in Juniper implies layer 2 switching, hence the Cisco switchport commands\\n4. If this were on a Cisco router (not switch), you would need to add \"no ip address\" to ensure it\\'s purely layer 2', 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'switchport trunk allowed vlan 10', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco IOS command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.0\\n switchport trunk allowed vlan 10\\n switchport mode trunk\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, we typically use subinterfaces with dot notation (Gi1/0/0.0)\\n2. The command enables trunking mode and allows only VLAN 10\\n3. The \"family ethernet-switching\" in Juniper implies layer 2 switching, hence the Cisco switchport commands\\n4. If this were on a Cisco router (not switch), you would need to add \"no ip address\" to ensure it\\'s purely layer 2', 'trans_template': 'switchport trunk allowed vlan 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'switchport mode trunk', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface range [parameter1] - [parameter2]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco IOS command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.0\\n switchport trunk allowed vlan 10\\n switchport mode trunk\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, we typically use subinterfaces with dot notation (Gi1/0/0.0)\\n2. The command enables trunking mode and allows only VLAN 10\\n3. The \"family ethernet-switching\" in Juniper implies layer 2 switching, hence the Cisco switchport commands\\n4. If this were on a Cisco router (not switch), you would need to add \"no ip address\" to ensure it\\'s purely layer 2', 'trans_template': 'switchport mode trunk'}]]": 1,
    "['unit [parameter2] encapsulation vlan-ccc', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.10', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.10\\n encapsulation dot1q 10\\n xconnect 1.1.1.1 10 encapsulation mpls\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper command creates a VLAN CCC (Circuit Cross Connect) subinterface with VLAN ID 10\\n2. The Cisco equivalent:\\n   - Creates a subinterface with dot1q encapsulation for VLAN 10\\n   - Sets up an MPLS pseudowire (xconnect) to the router ID 1.1.1.1 with a VC ID of 10 (assuming this is part of the VPLS configuration)\\n   - The VC ID (10) matches the VLAN ID for consistency, though in real deployments this might be configured differently\\n\\nNote: The exact xconnect parameters might vary depending on the full VPLS/L2VPN context not fully shown in the provided configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'encapsulation dot1q 10', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.10\\n encapsulation dot1q 10\\n xconnect 1.1.1.1 10 encapsulation mpls\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper command creates a VLAN CCC (Circuit Cross Connect) subinterface with VLAN ID 10\\n2. The Cisco equivalent:\\n   - Creates a subinterface with dot1q encapsulation for VLAN 10\\n   - Sets up an MPLS pseudowire (xconnect) to the router ID 1.1.1.1 with a VC ID of 10 (assuming this is part of the VPLS configuration)\\n   - The VC ID (10) matches the VLAN ID for consistency, though in real deployments this might be configured differently\\n\\nNote: The exact xconnect parameters might vary depending on the full VPLS/L2VPN context not fully shown in the provided configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'encapsulation dot1q 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'xconnect 1.1.1.1 10 encapsulation mpls', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0.10\\n encapsulation dot1q 10\\n xconnect 1.1.1.1 10 encapsulation mpls\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper command creates a VLAN CCC (Circuit Cross Connect) subinterface with VLAN ID 10\\n2. The Cisco equivalent:\\n   - Creates a subinterface with dot1q encapsulation for VLAN 10\\n   - Sets up an MPLS pseudowire (xconnect) to the router ID 1.1.1.1 with a VC ID of 10 (assuming this is part of the VPLS configuration)\\n   - The VC ID (10) matches the VLAN ID for consistency, though in real deployments this might be configured differently\\n\\nNote: The exact xconnect parameters might vary depending on the full VPLS/L2VPN context not fully shown in the provided configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'xconnect 1.1.1.1 10 encapsulation mpls'}]]": 1,
