    "['family l2vpn signaling', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp <AS_NUMBER>', 'parent_command': ['aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local'], 'root': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP L2VPN configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp <AS_NUMBER>\\n  neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> send-community both\\n  address-family l2vpn vpls\\n   neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> activate\\n##\\n  \\nNotes:\\n1. You\\'ll need to replace <AS_NUMBER> with your BGP autonomous system number\\n2. Replace <NEIGHBOR_IP> with the actual neighbor IP (2.2.2.2 in the original Juniper config)\\n3. The \"send-community both\" is typically required for L2VPN signaling to work properly in Cisco\\n4. This assumes you\\'ve already configured the basic BGP session parameters (like neighbor IP and remote-as)', 'trans_template': 'router bgp <AS_NUMBER>'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> send-community both', 'parent_command': ['aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local'], 'root': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP L2VPN configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp <AS_NUMBER>\\n  neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> send-community both\\n  address-family l2vpn vpls\\n   neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> activate\\n##\\n  \\nNotes:\\n1. You\\'ll need to replace <AS_NUMBER> with your BGP autonomous system number\\n2. Replace <NEIGHBOR_IP> with the actual neighbor IP (2.2.2.2 in the original Juniper config)\\n3. The \"send-community both\" is typically required for L2VPN signaling to work properly in Cisco\\n4. This assumes you\\'ve already configured the basic BGP session parameters (like neighbor IP and remote-as)', 'trans_template': 'neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> send-community both'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'address-family l2vpn vpls', 'parent_command': ['aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local'], 'root': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP L2VPN configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp <AS_NUMBER>\\n  neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> send-community both\\n  address-family l2vpn vpls\\n   neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> activate\\n##\\n  \\nNotes:\\n1. You\\'ll need to replace <AS_NUMBER> with your BGP autonomous system number\\n2. Replace <NEIGHBOR_IP> with the actual neighbor IP (2.2.2.2 in the original Juniper config)\\n3. The \"send-community both\" is typically required for L2VPN signaling to work properly in Cisco\\n4. This assumes you\\'ve already configured the basic BGP session parameters (like neighbor IP and remote-as)', 'trans_template': 'address-family l2vpn vpls'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> activate', 'parent_command': ['aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local'], 'root': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP L2VPN configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp <AS_NUMBER>\\n  neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> send-community both\\n  address-family l2vpn vpls\\n   neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> activate\\n##\\n  \\nNotes:\\n1. You\\'ll need to replace <AS_NUMBER> with your BGP autonomous system number\\n2. Replace <NEIGHBOR_IP> with the actual neighbor IP (2.2.2.2 in the original Juniper config)\\n3. The \"send-community both\" is typically required for L2VPN signaling to work properly in Cisco\\n4. This assumes you\\'ve already configured the basic BGP session parameters (like neighbor IP and remote-as)', 'trans_template': 'neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP> activate'}]]": 1,
    "['set protocols bgp group [group_name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp <AS_NUMBER>', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router bgp <AS_NUMBER>', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp <AS_NUMBER>\\n neighbor vpls update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. You\\'ll need to replace `<AS_NUMBER>` with the actual BGP Autonomous System number that would be configured in the Cisco router.\\n2. In Cisco, we typically use the loopback interface (Loopback0) as the source address rather than specifying the IP directly.\\n3. The command assumes you\\'ve already configured a loopback interface with IP address 1.1.1.1 elsewhere in the configuration.\\n4. The neighbor group name \"vpls\" is preserved from the original configuration.', 'trans_template': 'router bgp <AS_NUMBER>'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor vpls update-source Loopback0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'neighbor vpls update-source Loopback0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp <AS_NUMBER>\\n neighbor vpls update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. You\\'ll need to replace `<AS_NUMBER>` with the actual BGP Autonomous System number that would be configured in the Cisco router.\\n2. In Cisco, we typically use the loopback interface (Loopback0) as the source address rather than specifying the IP directly.\\n3. The command assumes you\\'ve already configured a loopback interface with IP address 1.1.1.1 elsewhere in the configuration.\\n4. The neighbor group name \"vpls\" is preserved from the original configuration.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor vpls update-source Loopback0'}]]": 1,
    "['local-address [ip_address]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp <AS_NUMBER>', 'parent_command': ['router bgp <AS_NUMBER>'], 'root': 'router bgp <AS_NUMBER>', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp <AS_NUMBER>\\n neighbor vpls update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. `<AS_NUMBER>` should be replaced with your actual BGP autonomous system number (not shown in the original Juniper config)\\n2. In Cisco, we typically use loopback interfaces as the source for BGP sessions. Assuming 1.1.1.1 is configured on Loopback0 (similar to lo0.0 in Juniper)\\n3. The \"group vpls\" concept from Juniper is represented through the neighbor configuration in Cisco\\n4. The specific neighbor address (2.2.2.2) would be configured separately in Cisco with a `neighbor 2.2.2.2 remote-as <AS_NUMBER>` command', 'trans_template': 'router bgp <AS_NUMBER>'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor vpls update-source Loopback0', 'parent_command': ['router bgp <AS_NUMBER>'], 'root': 'router bgp <AS_NUMBER>', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp <AS_NUMBER>\\n neighbor vpls update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. `<AS_NUMBER>` should be replaced with your actual BGP autonomous system number (not shown in the original Juniper config)\\n2. In Cisco, we typically use loopback interfaces as the source for BGP sessions. Assuming 1.1.1.1 is configured on Loopback0 (similar to lo0.0 in Juniper)\\n3. The \"group vpls\" concept from Juniper is represented through the neighbor configuration in Cisco\\n4. The specific neighbor address (2.2.2.2) would be configured separately in Cisco with a `neighbor 2.2.2.2 remote-as <AS_NUMBER>` command', 'trans_template': 'neighbor vpls update-source Loopback0'}]]": 1,
